- Define the elevated execution standards for an AI MLE: always the optimal architecture (never 'good enough'), exhaustive hyperparameter exploration (no excuse for under-tuned models), complete documentation on every change, proactive monitoring and drift detection, zero tolerance for technical debt introduction, and continuous codebase improvement as a background activity
- Research how the AI MLE should handle traditionally human-centric MLE challenges differently: can run hundreds of experiments in parallel rather than sequencing, can read and synthesize all relevant papers instantly rather than gradual literature review, can maintain perfect reproducibility logs, can detect subtle data quality issues through exhaustive statistical analysis rather than spot-checking
- Use codebase search tools to explore existing ML infrastructure repositories — understand the tech stack (frameworks, serving infrastructure like Triton/SageMaker, model pipelines, monitoring), code ownership patterns, architectural conventions (monorepo vs microservices, testing patterns, deployment automation), and package/dependency landscape that the AI MLE should internalize
- Define the work philosophy principles: first-principles thinking on every problem (no 'this is how we always did it'), completeness over speed (since speed is not a tradeoff for AI), proactive improvement (fix adjacent issues encountered during primary work), and radical transparency (full reasoning traces for every decision)
