# Core MLE Role Definition: Comprehensive Research Report

## Executive Summary

This report synthesizes internal Atlassian documentation, job descriptions (P40–P70levels), ML platform architecture documents, the "Digital MLE: AI Employee Vision" page, talent landscape research, and operational frameworks to define the complete MLE role. The research covers the full lifecycle from data processing through production monitoring, catalogs tools and platforms, maps domain expertise areas, and enumerates the operational artifacts MLEs produce and maintain.

---

## 1. Core Day-to-Day Responsibilities

Based on Atlassian's MLE role profile (grounded in the [Digital MLE: AI Employee Vision](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202027e1af5d30e94112880666ee62f9277a/pages/7051610727/Digital+MLE+AI+Employee+Vision) and multiple JD levels), the MLE role spans five core responsibility areas:

### 1.1 Data & Signal Engineering
- Identifies, extracts, and normalizes features from raw signals
- Manages data pipelines and refresh cadences
- Ensures training/serving consistency (detecting training/serving skew)
- Monitors signal freshness against SLA
- Detects feature drift and sparse/missing features by connector type
- Manages tenant-level data filtering (opt-in/opt-out governance)

### 1.2 Model Development & Training
- Designs experiments, selects architectures, trains models on labeled data
- Iterates based on evaluation results
- Runs scheduled retraining within defined cadences
- Generates training run summaries
- Compares model versions on offline metrics
- Manages model artifact versioning
- Flags when model age exceeds refresh policy

### 1.3 Evaluation & Experimentation
- **Offline evaluation**: Runs SxS (side-by-side) evaluations on holdout sets, interprets MRR/NDCG/precision metrics, identifies win/loss patterns
- **Online experimentation**: Designs A/B experiments, monitors Statsig scorecards, interprets metric movements, makes launch/rollback recommendations
- Gates flight promotion based on regression detection against baselines
- Extracts top win/loss query examples for interpretation

### 1.4 Deployment & Inference Serving
- Manages model serving infrastructure (TEAMServe/Triton, SageMaker)
- Model packaging for deployment (Triton config, conda packages, model registry upload)
- Progressive rollout management (10% → 50% → 100% ramp)
- Inference latency and throughput optimization
- Coordinates ramp-up plans and shadow testing
- Containerized model deployment (Docker, Kubernetes)

### 1.5 Production Monitoring & Operations
- Monitors serving latency against KR targets
- Detects throughput anomalies
- Tracks ramp-up schedules
- Data distribution shift detection
- Model performance metric monitoring (degenerate feedback loops, concept drift)
- Incident response coordination
- Operational metrics: latency, reliability, availability dashboards
- Post-incident analysis and SLO management

### 1.6 Cross-Functional Collaboration
- Works closely with product managers to formulate ML problems end-to-end
- Collaborates with designers and developers on experience and analytics
- Communicates results to non-technical stakeholders
- Mentors junior ML engineers
- Provides technical leadership under ambiguity

---

## 2. Required Technical Skills & Competency Levels

Based on [Phase 1 Deep-Dive: Machine Learning Engineer (MLE)](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/TADT/pages/7026569052/Phase+1+Deep-Dive+Machine+Learning+Engineer+MLE), internal JDs, and hiring criteria:

### 2.1 Core Technical Skills

| Category | Skills | Competency Level |
|----------|--------|-----------------|
| **Languages** | Python (primary, production-quality), Java/Kotlin (secondary), SQL, Go/TypeScript (bonus) | Expert in Python; proficient in at least one systems language |
| **ML Frameworks** | PyTorch (dominant), TensorFlow, JAX, scikit-learn, Keras, NumPy, Pandas, HuggingFace Transformers | Expert in PyTorch; working knowledge of alternatives |
| **Mathematics** | Linear algebra, probability/statistics, optimization theory, calculus | Strong foundation (Masters/PhD-level quantitative reasoning) |
| **Data Engineering** | Spark/PySpark, SQL, streaming pipelines, Delta tables, Databricks | Proficient; can build and maintain data pipelines at scale |
| **MLOps** | CI/CD for ML (Bitbucket Pipelines), containerization (Docker), orchestration (Kubernetes/KITT), model versioning | Proficient; can implement end-to-end deployment pipelines |
| **Production ML** | Data pipelines, model training, evaluation, deployment, productionization at scale | Expert; end-to-end ownership expected |

### 2.2 In-Demand Niche Skills (2026 Market)

| Category | Skills |
|----------|--------|
| **GenAI & LLMs** | LLMs, Generative AI, fine-tuning (LoRA, PEFT, SFT), evaluation, AI safety, prompt engineering |
| **MLOps & Infra** | MLflow, Tecton/MLFS, Pinecone, feature stores, AWS SageMaker, Databricks, TEAMServe/Triton |
| **Applied Domains** | Search & ranking systems, cloud ML infrastructure, AIOps, agentic AI |
| **Rarest skills (2026)** | Agentic AI, LLM evaluation/safety, distributed training at scale |

### 2.3 Soft Skills & Competencies

| Category | Skills |
|----------|--------|
| **Collaboration** | Cross-functional collaboration, stakeholder management, end-to-end ownership |
| **Leadership** | Mentorship, technical leadership, strong decision-making under ambiguity |
| **Communication** | Research communication, publishing, ability to explain data science concepts to diverse audiences |
| **Mindset** | 80/20 pragmatism, agile iteration, business impact focus |

### 2.4 Level Calibration (Atlassian-Specific)

| Level | Experience | Key Differentiators |
|-------|-----------|---------------------|
| **P40** | 2–4 years (typical4–8) | Executes ML workflows end-to-end with guidance |
| **P50** | 3–6 years (typical 6–9) | Independent end-to-end ownership, mentors juniors |
| **P60** | 5+ years | Architecture, scale, end-to-end ownership, leadership across teams |
| **P70** | 10+ years | Steers organization-wide ML strategy, defines technical direction |

---

## 3. Tools, Platforms, and Technologies

### 3.1 Experiment Tracking & Model Registry

| Tool | Purpose | Status at Atlassian |
|------|---------|-------------------|
| **MLflow** | Experiment tracking, training run logging, model registry, artifact management | Integrated with Databricks; primary experiment tracking system ([MLflow Overview](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/agd/pages/5795994519/MLflow+Overview)) |
| **Tarot** | Model registry (v2/v3) for model artifact storage and versioning | Internal; used for TEAMServe deployment pipeline |
| **W&B (Weights & Biases)** | Experiment tracking, loss curves, hyperparameter logging | Used alongside MLflow for specific use cases |

### 3.2 Feature Engineering & Data Platforms

| Tool | Purpose | Status |
|------|---------|--------|
| **Tecton** | Feature store — feature engineering, serving, real-time/batch/streaming features | Being migrated to MLFS; current production system ([Tecton Integration](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~63466de6b380bb46a09106ee/pages/3429509382/Tecton+Integration)) |
| **MLFS (ML Feature Store)** | Next-gen feature store replacing Tecton | In migration; backed by ERS (online) and Delta Lake (offline) |
| **Databricks/Socrates** | Data lake, analytics platform, ML experimentation workspace | Primary data platform |
| **Unified Feature Catalog** | SSOT for feature metadata via YAML configs in Bitbucket | Under development ([Unified Feature Catalog](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/DTIE/pages/6202634504)) |

### 3.3 Model Serving & Inference Infrastructure

| Tool | Purpose | Status |
|------|---------|--------|
| **TEAMServe** | Self-hosted AI inference platform using AWS EC2 + Kubernetes + Triton | Primary production serving; multi-region, multi-GPU ([TEAMServe](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202027e1af5d30e94112880666ee62f9277a/pages/3890943842/TEAMServe)) |
| **NVIDIA Triton Inference Server** | Multi-framework model serving (PyTorch, TensorRT, ONNX, vLLM) | Core inference runtime inside TEAMServe |
| **vLLM** | High-throughput LLM inference with Multi-LoRA support | Used for LLM serving |
| **TensorRT-LLM** | Optimized LLM inference with NVIDIA GPUs | Used for latency-critical LLM workloads |
| **AWS SageMaker (MSP)** | Model Serving Platform — being migrated to TEAMServe | Legacy; ~59% cost savings via migration |
| **NVIDIA GPUs** | H100, A100, g5 instances for training and inference | All GPU infrastructure is NVIDIA |

### 3.4 Training Infrastructure

| Tool | Purpose | Status |
|------|---------|--------|
| **ML Studio** | Atlassian's internal ML development platform — end-to-end workflows | Primary development platform ([ML Studio](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~712020b392f1b430fd427da35485751be61328/pages/6379096434/ML+Studio)) |
| **ML Fabric** | Interactive ML runtime for experimentation | Used for iterative development |
| **LLaMA-Factory** | LoRA/full fine-tuning framework for LLMs | Primary fine-tuning tool ([MLP Training Module](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202071ba312a6d8a4e95bdfcbf6226f44076/pages/5547755013/MLP+Training+Module+Notes)) |
| **DeepSpeed** | Distributed training (FSDP) for large models | Used for multi-GPU/multi-node training |
| **torchrun** | Multi-node training coordination | Standard distributed training launcher |
| **Databricks Clusters** | GPU compute for training | Primary training compute |

### 3.5 Orchestration & CI/CD

| Tool | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| **ML Studio Workflows** | YAML-based workflow definitions for training, eval, inference, deployment |
| **Bitbucket Pipelines** | CI/CD for ML code, model packaging, deployment |
| **Databricks Workflows** | Scheduled pipeline execution |
| **ArgoCD** | GitOps-based deployment for TEAMServe |
| **KITT (Kubernetes)** | Container orchestration infrastructure |
| **Karpenter** | GPU node autoscaling |

### 3.6 Monitoring & Observability

| Tool | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| **Statsig** | A/B experiment scorecards, feature flags, progressive rollout |
| **ML Lens** | Feature quality monitoring and observability |
| **SignalFx** | Detectors-as-code, real-time alerting |
| **Splunk** | Log analysis and operational monitoring |
| **Custom Databricks Dashboards** | Feature quality DoD/WoW trends |
| **OpsGenie** | Incident alerting and on-call management |

### 3.7 LLM & GenAI Stack

| Tool | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| **AI Gateway** | Unified API for LLM access (OpenAI, Anthropic, Google, Bedrock, MSP) |
| **HuggingFace** | Model hub, transformers library, PEFT |
| **LangChain/LlamaIndex** | RAG and agent frameworks |
| **Fireworks AI** | External LLM inference |

