# Operational Framework for the Machine Learning Engineer (MLE) Role

## Comprehensive Research Report

---

## 1. Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs)

### 1.1 Model Validation & Deployment Pipeline

Based on established practices across Atlassian ML teams and industry-standard MLOps frameworks (aligned with Gartner's MLOps Framework and Microsoft's MLOps Maturity Model), the MLE deployment pipeline follows a progressive validation approach:

**Stage 1: Offline Evaluation**
- Model trained and evaluated against holdout datasets using offline metrics (see §2for specifics)
- Experiment tracked in MLflow/ML Studio with full reproducibility — training code, data versions, hyperparameters, and resulting model artifacts are version controlled
- Exit criteria: Model meets or exceeds baseline performance on key metrics (Precision, Recall, F1, NDCG@K as applicable)
- Both training code and resulting models must be version controlled (MUST HAVE per [MLOps Framework](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/VAPPDEV/pages/2166657676/Machine+Learning+Operations+MLOps+Framework))

**Stage 2: Model Health Check / Unit Validation**
- Automated post-training health check pipeline with three stages: artifact existence verification, model loadability in target serving environment, and canary inference (small golden request set, asserting non-null, structurally valid predictions, no runtime exceptions) — per [AIOPS-5946](https://hello.jira.atlassian.cloud/browse/AIOPS-5946)
- Launch validation via Launchpad: model deploys to TeamServe, waits for `InService` status, sends golden request, records validation annotation in ML Registry ([Design Doc: ts_lp_launch_validation](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~7120203e8fad871e7f452c8bec69de58960c46/pages/6962155706))
- Load testing via perfhammer: benchmark latency against KR targets before promotion

**Stage 3: Shadow Mode / Shadow Deployment**
- Model serves predictions in parallel with production but does NOT serve to users
- Real online data replayed through new model for comparison ("Model Shadow Testing" in ML Lens roadmap — [Platformized ML Observability](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/5362077533))
- Enables RCA and model development by allowing MLEs to reproduce real production scenarios
- Shadow A/B: Clone search requests to experimental cluster without user impact ([HOWTO: Online Index Experiment](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SER/pages/4516580173))

**Stage 4: Canary Deployment**
- Progressive rollout with automated canary analysis (Netflix three-cluster model: production, baseline, canary)
- Traffic split incrementally (e.g., 10% → 50% → 100%) with soak time at each stage
- Automated analysis compares canary vs baseline using health, error rate, and latency detectors
- Auto-rollback if canary score drops below threshold (marginalScore: 50, passScore: 70) — [Canary Deployment Implementation Guide](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~7120203751105336c748498809ee74d33626f3/pages/6372694534)
- SLA-aware canary approach validates code identical to shipped code in production subset

**Stage 5: Full Production Deployment**
- Model pre-launch checklist must be completed
- Announcement to stakeholders, production capacity confirmed
- Continuous monitoring of serving metrics post-deployment
- Pause deployment if SLO breach detected (reliability, latency)

### 1.2 A/B Testing Methodology

Atlassian uses **Statsig** via **Switcheroo** (Atlassian's wrapper for safe rollouts) for online experimentation ([10,000 - Experimentation](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SER/pages/6589879032)):

- **Experiment Design**: Hypothesis-driven with predefined success criteria, primary and secondary metrics
- **Randomization**: Feature flags evaluated last after all eligibility conditions; multiple flags used if necessary to prevent exposure event bias
- **Two types**: Guardrail experiments (ship feature, verify no negative impact) and Hypothesis experiments (A/B test measuring specific product/team metrics)
- **Process**: Design & Alignment → Implementation & Launch → Monitoring & Analysis → Review & Decision → Documentation
- **Analysis**: Statsig scorecard with 95% significance thresholds and pre-registered metrics
- **Post-analysis**: Present at Weekly Experimentation Review (WEX) before shipping non-control cohorts

### 1.3 Bias and Fairness Auditing

Per Atlassian's AI Safety & Responsible AI framework ([9 — AI Safety & Responsible AI](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~7120201bc0c38992be4219ba741180eb8a6969/pages/7012229938)):
- Test for fairness and harmful bias where outputs affect people or decisions
- Evaluate on diverse representative inputs, measure outputs across demographic groups
- Use red-teaming to probe for unsafe behavior
- Document known limitations in model cards
- Mandatory Responsible Technology Review before production deployment
- Register with AI Gateway and complete Responsible AI review ([ITG: AI Security & Responsible Use](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~187250055/pages/6907793108))

### 1.4 Rollback Procedures

- **Automated rollback**: Canary analysis triggers immediate rollback if health score drops below marginal threshold (50/100)
- **Safe-rollback wrapper script**: Preserves ASG floor during rollback to prevent capacity loss
- **Circuit breaker pattern**: Time-based sliding window (60s), opens at≥20% failure rate on 5xx errors, exponential backoff with max 60s wait — [ML-Engine Circuit Breaker Runbook](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/ITSOL/pages/6058351626)
- **Fail-open kill-switch**: For moderation services, fail-open mode ensures service availability even when model path fails (per Tony's own [OPP-202605-02])
- Model deployment remains manual after retraining for now (human verification before promotion) — [RFC: Model Re-training](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/6013257858)

### 1.5 Data Versioning & Experiment Hygiene

- Data tagged as training or validation; models versioned to ensure measured accuracy is on unseen data
- ML Studio automatically creates/reuses experiments per workflow run; full configuration and metrics tracked
- Data pipeline gathers data automatically (MUST HAVE at MLOps Level 3)
- Feature Service versioning: DRAFT v2 exists alongside production v1 without side effects ([How to create a new version of a Feature Service](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/6157611964))
- Praxis-ML: Interactive and production paths share same recipes, configs, and submission model to minimize drift ([Praxis-ML One-Pager](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/es33tss22axj/pages/7003858857))

