## 6. Team Structure and Organizational Fit

### 6.1 Where the MLE Sits

Based on multiple Atlassian org structures observed:

**Primary Structure**: The Core Machine Learning team sits within the **Data Technology Insights (DTI)** group in the **Corporate Engineering Organization** ([P40 MLE](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~455370031/pages/4678981237), [REQ-2025-4613](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~455370031/pages/5762266848)).

**Alternative Placements** observed across Atlassian:
- **Product-embedded MLEs**: Confluence team, Search & Relevance, Jira Align (close to product, own model lifecycle end-to-end)
- **Platform MLEs**: ML Platform team, ML Studio, ML Fabric (build infrastructure for other MLEs)
- **Domain-specific**: AIOps (JSM), AI Safety Engineering, Growth Intelligence
- **AI Lab / Product AI**: Experimental/research-oriented teams (e.g., Eng - Product AI where Tony sits)

### 6.2 Team Composition

The ML Platform team's proposed structure ([Machine Learning Platform - Primer](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/851881271)):

| Team | Focus |
|------|-------|
| **ML Tools** | Build/maintain core ML Platform capabilities for developing, training, evaluating, deploying, and operating ML models and pipelines |
| **MLOps** | Deploy and operate models for teams that don't have dedicated MLEs/Data Scientists |
| **MLaaS** | Build/maintain systems for engineers to train and operate high-quality models without deep ML knowledge |

### 6.3 MLE vs. ML Systems Engineer

Key distinction per [MLSE Lunch and Learning](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~70121fa4d12f14797451589839ddf71b9d027/pages/4910851230):
- **MLE**: Model development, algorithm implementation, experimentation, evaluation, cross-functional with product/analytics. Focus on ML fundamentals, PyTorch/TensorFlow, business applications at scale.
- **MLSE**: Infrastructure underpinning ML systems — backend architecture, scalability, distributed systems (AWS), data processing (Spark). Less model expertise, more system engineering.

### 6.4 The Bridge Role

The MLE uniquely bridges:
- **Research↔ Production**: Converts DS experimentation code into repeatable, scalable production systems
- **Data ↔ Product**: Translates business problems into technical solutions and back
- **Platform ↔ Application**: Leverages platform primitives (ML Studio, TeamServe, ML Fabric) to build domain-specific ML applications
- **Offline ↔ Online**: Ensures offline experimental results translate to online impact via proper A/B methodology

### 6.5 Reporting Relationships

- **IC MLE (P40-P50)**: Reports to Engineering Manager or Principal Engineer
- **Senior MLE (P50-P60)**: Reports to EM; provides guidance to junior MLEs; expected to mentor
- **Principal/Sr. Principal MLE (P60-P70)**: Reports to Director/VP; steers technical direction; cross-functional influence; mentoring and leadership

### 6.6 Cross-Functional Working Model

Per Atlassian's Core ML team: "We work cross-functionally across stakeholders in **Growth, GTM, Product, Finance and Sales organizations**, providing AIML applications. Our team consists of ML engineers and system engineers, and provides many opportunities for collaboration, knowledge sharing, and individual growth. We are highly nimble, with a **focus on velocity between conceptualization and initial output**, and **laser-focused on business impact**."

Key hiring signal: "Focus on business practicality and the80/20 rule; very high bar for output quality, but recognize the business benefit of 'having something now' vs'perfection sometime in the future'" ([P70 MLE JD](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202049b56fb3ef7b49abbbe4f53dbbf12160/pages/3171033424))

