## Part 2: Codebase & Technical Context — The Infrastructure the AI MLE Must Internalize

### 2.1 ML Infrastructure Tech Stack at Atlassian

Based on extensive exploration of internal documentation, the Atlassian ML infrastructure stack consists of:

#### Compute & Training Layer
| Component | Role | Key Details |
|-----------|------|-------------|
| **ML Fabric** | Kubernetes-based training compute orchestration | Multi-node distributed training (PyTorch DDP, torchrun), Ray-based workloads, GPU clusters (H200, A100, A10G, L40S), Karpenter autoscaling, Kubeflow Training Operators |
| **ML Studio** | ML development platform (Databricks wrapper) | End-to-end ML workflows, experiment management, module-based architecture, automated versioning, CI/CD integration |
| **Praxis-ML** | Unified training framework | Bridges ML Fabric and ML Studio; recipes as stable APIs for both humans and agents; interactive-to-production paved path |
| **Argo Workflows** | Kubernetes-native workflow orchestration | Replacing Databricks workflows; GPU DAGs validated; WorkflowTemplates for reusability |
| **Kubeflow Pipelines (KFP)** | ML pipeline orchestration | Python SDK, typed ML artifacts, automatic MLMD lineage tracking, Vertex AI portable |

#### Serving & Inference Layer
| Component | Role | Key Details |
|-----------|------|-------------|
| **TeamServe** | In-house model-as-a-service platform | Built on Kubernetes (GKE/EKS) + ArgoCD; Triton Inference Server + TensorRT-LLM + vLLM backends; gRPC communications;72% latency reduction vs SageMaker; ~$5.6M/year cost savings |
| **Triton Inference Server** | Model serving runtime | Dynamic batching, model pipelines via ensembling, HTTP/REST and gRPC protocols, Python backend API, multi-framework support |
| **SageMaker** | Legacy AWS model hosting | Being migrated to TeamServe; still serves image-moderation-v0/v1 and some models in transition |
| **AI Gateway** | Centralized AI model routing | ASAP authentication, prompt moderation, model selection |

#### Experiment Tracking & Registry
| Component | Role | Key Details |
|-----------|------|-------------|
| **MLflow** | Experiment tracking and model registry | Integrated with Databricks and ML Fabric; artifact logging (models, checkpoints, logs); GPU metrics collection |
| **ML Registry v3** | Model artifact management | Namespace/component hierarchy, version management, atlassian_msp_sdk integration |
| **Statsig** | Online experimentation | Scorecard monitoring, feature flags, dynamic config, statistical significance assessment |

#### Monitoring & Observability
| Component | Role | Key Details |
|-----------|------|-------------|
| **SignalFx** | Detectors and alerting | Currently a major gap —0 detectors exist for responsible-ai-api; the AI MLE should build these as code (IaC) |
| **Splunk** | Centralized logging | Log aggregation for training jobs and serving infrastructure |
| **Prometheus** | Metrics collection | GPU utilization, inference latency per model, throughput, autoscaling behavior, queue depth |
| **Pollinator** | Synthetic health checks | Production endpoint validation |
| **OTel (OpenTelemetry)** | Distributed tracing | Request latency inspection across the serving stack |

#### Infrastructure & Deployment
| Component | Role | Key Details |
|-----------|------|-------------|
| **KITT** | Kubernetes infrastructure management | Managed clusters, Karpenter node provisioning, fleet management |
| **ArgoCD** | GitOps deployment | Syncs deployments to data plane, monitors health, Kustomize overlays |
| **Karpenter** | GPU node autoscaling | Manages GPU instance provisioning for training and inference |
| **Databricks** | Data platform (being migrated from) | Still provides data access layer; transitioning orchestration to Kubernetes-native |

### 2.2 Code Structure & Architectural Conventions

Based on the ML Fabrics Training Infrastructure documentation:

```
# Core ML Fabric Infrastructure
modules/src/ml_platform/ml_fabric_create_run/
├── src/ml_fabric_create_run/
│   ├── main.py              # Main orchestration (job submission, monitoring)
│   ├── kitt.py              # Kubernetes interface (PyTorchJob, RayJob creation)
│   ├── databricks.py        # Databricks integration (tokens, service principals)
│   ├── job.py               # Job monitoring (polling, Splunk links, state transitions)
│   ├── aws.py               # AWS instance registry
│   └── container.py         # Container setup

# Training Modules (diverse model types)
modules/src/ml_platform/mlp_llm_finetune/       # LLM fine-tuning
modules/src/ml_platform/mlp_verl_example/       # RL training
modules/src/loom_ai/speech_forced_aligner/      # Speech models
modules/src/search_relevance/                # Search ranking models

# Workflow Definitions
workflows/src/ai_assistant/glaze/              # GPT-OSS training
workflows/src/ai_modeling/nl2cypher/           # NL2Cypher fine-tuning
workflows/src/search_relevance/                # Search relevance models

# Triton Model Structure (for serving)
<model_name>/
├── config.pbtxt          # Triton model config (inputs/outputs/batching)
├── 1/                    # Version directory
│   ├── model.py         # Triton Python backend (TritonPythonModel)
│   ├── model.onnx       # ONNX weights
│   └── tokenizer files  # tokenizer.json, config.json, etc.
```

#### Key Architectural Patterns:
1. **Module-based development**: ML Studio organizes code into reusable modules (data processing, training, evaluation)
2. **Docker modules for GPU workloads**: ML Fabric only supports Docker modules for training
3. **Bitbucket CI/CD integration**: Library packaging and publishing through ML Studio's built-in pipeline
4. **ASAP authentication**: Service-to-service auth via Atlassian Service Authentication Protocol
5. **Feature flags via Statsig**: Gradual rollout, shadow testing, A/B experiments
6. **Circuit breaker pattern**: Used in responsible-ai-api for fault tolerance (fail_max thresholds)

### 2.3 The responsible-ai-api as a Reference System

Tony's recent work on the responsible-ai-api provides a concrete reference for ML infrastructure patterns the AI MLE should deeply understand:

**Architecture**: Stateless, real-time content moderation service on Micros platform
-4 moderation endpoints: prompt, agent, image, output
- Dependencies: Redis (caching), SageMaker (image models), AI Gateway (LLM models), TeamServe (gRPC inference), GASv3 (analytics), Statsig (feature flags), TCS (tenant context)

**Models in production**:
- Prompt moderation: Llama 3.1 8B (default) → GPT-OSS Safeguard 20B (rolling out)
- Agent moderation: GPT-4.1-mini (via AI Gateway)
- Image moderation: V0 (custom object detection on SageMaker) + V1 (ShieldGemma on SageMaker)
- Prompt moderation (TeamServe shadow): teamserve-rai-optimized-logits (gRPC)

**Known reliability challenges** (exactly what the AI MLE should fix):
- Gevent event-loop starvation from blocking gRPC calls → tail latency spikes (12-20s)
- No SignalFx detectors → incidents discovered by user report
- Stale runbook (~470 days old)
- SLOs not encoded in IaC
- Inconsistent fail-open/circuit-breaker behavior across endpoints
- Single-tenant burst can collapse image moderation endpoint

